The flake-detection script will throw and fail the workflow if `results.json` exists but is malformed/partial (e.g., Playwright process interrupted, file truncated). Since this step runs `if: always()`, a parse error could incorrectly turn an otherwise-successful run into a failed job. Consider wrapping the JSON read/parse in try/catch and defaulting outputs to `flaky=false` (or emitting a separate `flaky_parse_error` output) instead of letting the step fail.
```suggestion
          try {
            const raw = fs.readFileSync(file, "utf8")
            const report = JSON.parse(raw)
            const count = Number(report?.stats?.flaky ?? report?.suitesSummary?.flaky ?? 0)
            fs.appendFileSync(
              process.env.GITHUB_OUTPUT,
              `flaky=${count > 0}\nflaky_count=${count}\n`
            )
          } catch (error) {
            console.error("Failed to read or parse Playwright results.json for flake detection:", error)
            fs.appendFileSync(process.env.GITHUB_OUTPUT, "flaky=false\nflaky_count=0\n")
            process.exit(0)
          }
```

This test writes an `anthropic` OAuth entry but never removes/restores it. Since tests share the same XDG data directory for the whole run, leaving auth.json mutated can create order-dependent failures in other provider/auth tests. Please clean up (e.g., remove the key in a `try/finally`) or use a unique provider key for the test.
```suggestion
  const original = await Auth.get("anthropic")
  try {
    await Auth.set("anthropic", {
      type: "oauth",
      refresh: "refresh",
      access: "access",
      expires: 123,
      userAgent: "claude-code/2.1.76",
      billingHeader: "x-anthropic-billing-header: cc_version=2.1.76.4dc; cc_entrypoint=cli; cch=00000;",
    })

    const auth = await Auth.get("anthropic")
    expect(auth).toBeDefined()
    expect(auth?.type).toBe("oauth")
    if (auth?.type !== "oauth") return
    expect(auth.userAgent).toBe("claude-code/2.1.76")
    expect(auth.billingHeader).toBe(
      "x-anthropic-billing-header: cc_version=2.1.76.4dc; cc_entrypoint=cli; cch=00000;",
    )
  } finally {
    if (original !== undefined) {
      await Auth.set("anthropic", original)
    } else {
      await Auth.remove("anthropic")
    }
  }
```

You could simplify this a bit and avoid duplicating the logic by using a single target value (extension or basename).
Something like this:

```
export const matchesExtension = (file: string, extensions: string[]): boolean => {
  if (extensions.length === 0) return true

  const { ext, base } = path.parse(file)
  const target = (ext || base).toLowerCase()

  return extensions.some(e => e.toLowerCase() === target)
}
```

`max-width: calc(100% - 24px)` on `[data-slot="basic-tool-tool-trigger-content"]` permanently reserves space for the collapsible arrow, but the arrow is conditionally rendered (e.g., pending/locked/no children). In those cases this will unnecessarily truncate tool titles/subtitles even though the full width is available. Consider removing the hard max-width and instead rely on flex sizing (e.g., make trigger-content `flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0` and keep the arrow `flex-shrink: 0`) so the layout works whether the arrow is present or not.
```suggestion
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    width: auto;
```

This test creates a junction (`alias`) outside of `tmp.path`, but `tmpdir()` cleanup only removes `tmp.path`. That leaves an orphaned `*-alias` entry under the OS temp directory on Windows. Add explicit cleanup for `alias` (or create it inside `tmp.path`) to avoid leaking filesystem artifacts across test runs.
```suggestion
    try {
      const { project, sandbox } = await p.fromDirectory(alias)

      expect(String(project.worktree)).toBe(tmp.path)
      expect(String(sandbox)).toBe(tmp.path)
    } finally {
      // Ensure the alias junction is removed even if the test fails.
      await fs.unlink(alias).catch(() => {})
    }
```
